// Rejected. `corrupt` breaks `count <= len` with `target.count = 99`, then
// both arms of a complete dispatch return with the window still open. The
// trailing `crash Trap;` can never run. The checker used to accept this: any
// crash statement in a state turned off the end-of-state "return" window check
// (`domains_and_predicates.rs` `walk_state`). It now checks each returning row
// of the dispatch where the row runs, and only a crash row that ends the
// dispatch may abandon a window.
//
// Rule (wiki/spec/language/dependent_values.md, "Invariant windows"): the
// domain "must be proved again at the next consumption point", and "return"
// is one; "A crash may abandon an open window" covers only the crash outcome,
// not the returns beside it.
//
// History: accepted by a compiler built at c118c914794 on omega-mac
// (Jarods-Mac-mini.local), where `./target/debug/omega --check main.omg
// < /dev/null` exited 0 with no diagnostic, and pinned under
// `tests/omega/pending/dependent/` until the checker rejected it.
//
// Twin: the same program without `crash Trap;` and `crashes Trap` was already
// rejected with the diagnostic in `expected.txt`. The opposite case, a state
// whose only exit is a crash and which may leave the window open, is
// `pass/dependent/data_requires_crash_abandons_window`.
data Tally
requires
    count <= len,
{
    len: u32;
    count: u32;
}
machine corrupt(target: &mut Tally, ready: bool) crashes Trap {
    target.count = 99;
    transition ready { true -> {} false -> {} }
    crash Trap;
}
machine main() {}
